.SH NAME
ktm-guide-safety-and-debugging \- Safety and debugging (chapter 29 of the ktm language guide)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
ktm is a systems language: it has pointers and manual memory, and a program can still go wrong in the ways C programs do. What it adds is help in finding out where. This chapter covers the checks ktm makes while a program runs (bounds checks, zeroed locals), the reports a failing program prints (uncaught errors and crashes), finding memory bugs with \fB\-Memsafe\fR, looking at what the compiler made of a program, and using a native debugger.
.SH Bounds checks
.PP
Indexing a slice checks the index against its length; out of range, it throws \fBERR_BOUNDS\fR with the index, the length and where (see Arrays and slices (see ktm-guide-arrays-and-slices(7))):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i32 main() {
    i64 nums[3] = {1, 2, 3};
    i64[] s = nums;
    i64 i = 3;
    try {
        printf("%d\en", s[i]);
    } catch (Error e) {
        printf("%s (%s:%d)\en", e.message, e.file, e.line);
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
index 3 out of range for length 3 (example.ktm:6)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Slicing is checked the same way:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i32 main() {
    u8[] word = "hello";
    i64 end = 9;
    try {
        u8[] part = word[2:end];
        printf("%d\en", part.len);
    } catch (Error e) {
        printf("%s\en", e.message);
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
slice 2:9 out of range for length 5
.EE
.RE
.PP
The check is what makes a slice safer than a pointer: a pointer has no length, so \fBp[i]\fR on a plain pointer isn't checked. Prefer slices for anything indexed with a computed number.
.SH Locals start zeroed
.PP
A local variable declared without a value is zero: 0, 0.0, false, null, an empty \fBString\fR or slice, a struct or array with every byte 0. Reading one before setting it is never garbage:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Report {
    i64 lines;
    f64 ratio;
    u8 *name;
};

i32 main() {
    i64 n;
    Report r;
    i64 grid[4];
    printf("%d %d %.1f %d %d\en", n, r.lines, r.ratio, r.name == null, grid[3]);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
0 0 0.0 1 0
.EE
.RE
.SH Uncaught errors
.PP
An error no \fBtry\fR catches ends the program with exit status 1, after printing its code, message, where it was thrown, and the calls that led there (see Errors (see ktm-guide-errors(7))):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i64 parsePort(u8[] s) {
    if (s.len == 0) {
        throw(1, "empty port");
    }
    return 80;
}

i64 loadConfig() {
    return parsePort("");
}

i32 main() {
    printf("port %d\en", loadConfig());
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
uncaught error 1: empty port (example.ktm:3)
  at example.ktm:3 in parsePort
  called from example.ktm:9 in loadConfig (inlined)
  called from example.ktm:13 in main
.EE
.RE
.SH Crash reports
.PP
A program that crashes (a null pointer, an integer division by zero, a stack overflow, memory it doesn't own) prints what happened and where, with the calls that led there, before it dies of the signal as it would without the report:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Node {
    i64 value;
    Node *next;
};

i64 second(Node *n) {
    return n.next.value;
}

i32 main() {
    Node last = {2, null};
    printf("%d\en", second(&last));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
crash: null pointer dereference (address 0x0)
  at example.ktm:7 in second (inlined)
  called from example.ktm:12 in main
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBsecond\fR was inlined into \fBmain\fR by the compiler, and still shows as a call of its own, marked \fB(inlined)\fR\&.
.PP
An integer division by zero (\fB/\fR or \fB%\fR) crashes:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i64 average(i64 total, i64 count) {
    return total / count;
}

i32 main() {
    printf("%d\en", average(10, 2));
    i64 none = 0;
    printf("%d\en", average(10, none));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ktm avg.ktm \-o avg && ./avg
5
crash: integer division by zero
  at avg.ktm:2 in average (inlined)
  called from avg.ktm:8 in main
Floating point exception
.EE
.RE
.PP
What the program printed before the crash comes first. The arm64 processor doesn't trap a division by zero by itself, so there the compiler tests a divisor that can be zero before dividing: the crash, its report and the exit status (136, a SIGFPE) are the same on every platform. Check a divisor that can be zero before dividing when the program should go on. The smallest \fBi64\fR divided by -1 crashes the same way, with \fBcrash: integer overflow in division (\-9223372036854775808 / \-1)\fR\&.
.PP
A recursion that never ends overflows the stack, and shows once, with how many times it repeated:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i64 depth(i64 n) {
    i64 pad[64];
    pad[0] = n;
    return depth(n + 1) + pad[0];
}

i32 main() {
    printf("%d\en", depth(0));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ktm deep.ktm \-o deep && ./deep
crash: stack overflow: the calls below went too deep (a recursion that never ends?)
  at deep.ktm:1 in depth
  called from deep.ktm:4 in depth (14962 times)
  called from deep.ktm:4 in depth (inlined)
  called from deep.ktm:8 in main
Segmentation fault
.EE
.RE
.PP
Writing to a string literal is a crash too: literals live in read-only memory. Copy one with \fBstr.from\fR (or into a \fBu8\fR array) to change it.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cat lit.ktm
i32 main() {
    u8 *s = "literal";
    s[0] = 76;
    return 0;
}
$ ktm lit.ktm \-o lit && ./lit
crash: invalid memory access at address 0x562a92f81361 (memory that was freed, never allocated, or read\-only)
  at lit.ktm:3 in main
Segmentation fault
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Compiled programs and \fB\-jit\fR report the same way, in every thread.
.IP \(bu 2
A crash inside C code (\fBlibc.strlen(null)\fR) shows \fBin C code\fR, then the ktm line that called it and that line's callers, up to \fBmain\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
A package's function shows by the name the program calls it by (\fBpg.Pool.exec\fR), its file under the package's path.
.IP \(bu 2
The exit status is still the signal's: 139 for a segmentation fault, 136 for a division by zero on x86_64, as a shell reports them.
.IP \(bu 2
Programs carry a table of their functions' lines for the report, about a quarter of their size.
.SH Memory bugs: -Memsafe
.PP
\fBktm \-jit \-Memsafe\fR tracks every heap block while the program runs. Double frees and frees of memory that was never allocated are reported, and skipped, as they happen; blocks that leaked are listed when \fBmain\fR returns or the program calls \fBexit()\fR\&. Each report names the line in your code:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import mem;

i32 main() {
    u8 *a = mem.alloc(16);
    u8 *b = mem.alloc(32);
    mem.free(a);
    mem.free(a);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ktm \-jit \-Memsafe leaky.ktm
Free: double free of 0x555e8dc678c8 (16 bytes)
  allocated   at leaky.ktm:4 in main
  first freed at leaky.ktm:6 in main
  this free   at leaky.ktm:7 in main \- ignored, heap unharmed
memsafe: 1 allocation(s) (32 bytes) never freed
  0x555e8dc67ea8        32 bytes  at leaky.ktm:5 in main
.EE
.RE
.PP
What the library allocates for you is tracked too, and reported at your line with the library function it went through:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

i32 main() {
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        String s = str.format("item %d", i);
        printf("%s\en", s);
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ktm \-jit \-Memsafe loop.ktm
item 0
item 1
item 2
memsafe: 3 allocation(s) (768 bytes) never freed
  0x5576d4d659c8       256 bytes  at loop.ktm:5 in main, via str.format
  0x5576d4d670d8       256 bytes  at loop.ktm:5 in main, via str.format
  0x5576d4d672c8       256 bytes  at loop.ktm:5 in main, via str.format
.EE
.RE
.PP
The fix is \fBscoped String s = ...\fR (see defer and scoped (see ktm-guide-defer-and-scoped(7))), and the report is empty.
.IP \(bu 2
A leak is a block nothing points to any more. What a global, the library, or (when the program calls \fBexit()\fR) a live variable still holds at exit is in use, not a leak: it is listed apart, so a program that ends while holding its data needs no frees just for the report.
.IP \(bu 2
The check is conservative: a word that looks like a pointer keeps a block alive.
.IP \(bu 2
It works in any thread. The REPL always tracks allocations: \fBreplHeap();\fR lists the live ones.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-Memsafe\fR is for the JIT; a test suite runs it with \fBktm test \-jit \-Memsafe\fR\&.
.SH Looking under the hood
.PP
Each stage of the compiler can be shown:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ktm \-tokens main.ktm         # what the lexer sees
$ ktm \-ast main.ktm            # the parsed program
$ ktm \-\-dump\-ir main.ktm       # the SSA intermediate representation
$ ktm \-S \-o\- main.ktm          # the generated assembly
$ ktm \-\-expand main.ktm        # the code @json and @yaml wrote
$ ktm \-cfg main.ktm && dot \-Tpng main.dot \-o main.png   # a control flow graph
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fB\-cfg\-png\fR and \fB\-cfg\-svg\fR write the image directly (they need Graphviz). \fBtypeof(x)\fR prints a type inside a program:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i32 main() {
    auto n = 3;
    auto ratio = n / 2.0;
    u8 *name = "x";
    printf("%s %s %s\en", typeof(n), typeof(ratio), typeof(&name));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
i64 f64 u8 **
.EE
.RE
.SH Native debuggers
.PP
A compiled ktm program is a normal executable: \fBgdb\fR and \fBlldb\fR can run it, set breakpoints on function names, step through it and show registers and memory.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ktm app.ktm \-o app
$ gdb ./app
(gdb) break main
(gdb) run
.EE
.RE
.PP
A module's functions have their own symbols, \fB_K\fR followed by the module and name (\fBgeo.area\fR is \fB_K3geo4area\fR), so break on that name.
.SH Rules
.IP \(bu 2
Slice indexes and slices are bounds-checked and throw \fBERR_BOUNDS\fR; pointer indexes aren't.
.IP \(bu 2
Locals start zeroed.
.IP \(bu 2
An uncaught error prints its place and calls and exits with 1; a crash prints what happened and the calls, then dies of its signal. Both work compiled and in the JIT.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBktm \-jit \-Memsafe\fR reports double frees, wild frees and leaks at your lines.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-tokens\fR, \fB\-ast\fR, \fB\-\-dump\-ir\fR, \fB\-S\fR, \fB\-\-expand\fR and \fB\-cfg\fR show the compiler's stages; compiled programs work with gdb and lldb.
